1
00:00:01,517 --> 00:00:05,216
- So I'm at Eurostar, I was thinking about

2
00:00:05,216 --> 00:00:08,431
what lightning talk I'm
going to do this morning.

3
00:00:08,431 --> 00:00:10,888
And yesterday and a couple of days over

4
00:00:10,888 --> 00:00:12,159
the conference I've been getting certain

5
00:00:12,159 --> 00:00:13,188
questions coming through.

6
00:00:13,188 --> 00:00:15,069
So what I'm going to talk about is

7
00:00:15,069 --> 00:00:17,821
what testers can do and an easy way

8
00:00:17,821 --> 00:00:20,085
on Agile projects to help themselves.

9
00:00:20,085 --> 00:00:22,283
I will stick the timer
on for four minutes,

10
00:00:22,283 --> 00:00:23,432
I'm going to work from my notes

11
00:00:23,432 --> 00:00:25,765
and we'll see how far I get.

12
00:00:29,434 --> 00:00:31,851
So generally what I'm asked is,

13
00:00:31,851 --> 00:00:36,043
"Our Agile project is
screwed up. What can we do?"

14
00:00:36,043 --> 00:00:37,866
And I do get asked this a lot.

15
00:00:37,866 --> 00:00:39,955
LinkedIn, various other places, a couple

16
00:00:39,955 --> 00:00:41,476
people at the conference have asked.

17
00:00:41,476 --> 00:00:42,937
And what screwed up means,

18
00:00:42,937 --> 00:00:45,160
we don't have any time for testing.

19
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:46,231
By the testers.

20
00:00:46,231 --> 00:00:50,662
We've got no time for
automating, again by the testers.

21
00:00:50,662 --> 00:00:53,176
People are not skilled
enough doing the work,

22
00:00:53,176 --> 00:00:55,201
we don't know how to test this.

23
00:00:55,201 --> 00:00:56,885
We've got no acceptance criteria.

24
00:00:56,885 --> 00:00:58,891
That's good enough for the testers.

25
00:00:58,891 --> 00:01:02,974
There's no story and details,
we don't invited to meetings.

26
00:01:02,974 --> 00:01:04,615
And again this is all tester related.

27
00:01:04,615 --> 00:01:06,864
It's not that these
things aren't happening,

28
00:01:06,864 --> 00:01:09,528
it's just everything here
is related to the test part.

29
00:01:09,528 --> 00:01:11,752
And the problem here is that the projects

30
00:01:11,752 --> 00:01:14,103
themselves are not Agile.

31
00:01:14,103 --> 00:01:16,094
They're calling themselves Agile.

32
00:01:16,094 --> 00:01:19,130
They use Agile words,
they have Agile rituals

33
00:01:19,130 --> 00:01:23,710
in there: retrospectives,
stand ups and three amigos

34
00:01:23,710 --> 00:01:26,774
whatever else but there's
no agility in there.

35
00:01:26,774 --> 00:01:29,225
There's no attempt to continually improve

36
00:01:29,225 --> 00:01:30,611
and refine the system.

37
00:01:30,611 --> 00:01:33,641
So we have roles and
subsystems and testing,

38
00:01:33,641 --> 00:01:36,292
anything with testing it
is done by the testers.

39
00:01:36,292 --> 00:01:37,792
This is a problem.

40
00:01:38,739 --> 00:01:41,740
Now, we have to keep
fighting the good fight

41
00:01:41,740 --> 00:01:43,446
which is explaining what Agile is,

42
00:01:43,446 --> 00:01:45,374
explaining what testing is within Agile.

43
00:01:45,374 --> 00:01:48,977
But we also have to deal with
the actual reality we face.

44
00:01:48,977 --> 00:01:51,800
So some tactics for doing
that are first of all,

45
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:54,561
control the things that
you have control over.

46
00:01:54,561 --> 00:01:56,479
Right, so get your own house in order.

47
00:01:56,479 --> 00:02:01,308
Make sure that you have the
skills to do your testing.

48
00:02:01,308 --> 00:02:03,462
You've set the expectations you know what

49
00:02:03,462 --> 00:02:05,285
expectations people have
and you've agreed them.

50
00:02:05,285 --> 00:02:07,421
You avoid waste in your process.

51
00:02:07,421 --> 00:02:09,457
You're not writing big test plans.

52
00:02:09,457 --> 00:02:11,851
You keep things as lean as possible.

53
00:02:11,851 --> 00:02:14,567
Keep things documented
as lightly as possible,

54
00:02:14,567 --> 00:02:16,314
at the point that you need it.

55
00:02:16,314 --> 00:02:19,576
Keep it lean, and keep control.

56
00:02:19,576 --> 00:02:21,666
Own the things that are yours to own.

57
00:02:21,666 --> 00:02:25,069
So own exploratory
testing, and do that well.

58
00:02:25,069 --> 00:02:26,752
Make sure you do your exploratory testing,

59
00:02:26,752 --> 00:02:30,158
you write down your plan of
what you're gonna attack.

60
00:02:30,158 --> 00:02:33,171
That way, if you use Agile words like

61
00:02:33,171 --> 00:02:37,323
done and not done, when you've got a plan.

62
00:02:37,323 --> 00:02:40,388
When people come to say
"This story is done."

63
00:02:40,388 --> 00:02:42,611
and see that your testing
has got all the stuff

64
00:02:42,611 --> 00:02:44,277
that's done with all the evidence

65
00:02:44,277 --> 00:02:46,373
and all the steps that you took.

66
00:02:46,373 --> 00:02:48,345
But it's also got these
things that are not done,

67
00:02:48,345 --> 00:02:49,621
'cause you haven't had time.

68
00:02:49,621 --> 00:02:52,155
It's harder for them to ignore

69
00:02:52,155 --> 00:02:54,180
that there's a problem.

70
00:02:54,180 --> 00:02:56,577
'Cause that feedback is
staring them in the face,

71
00:02:56,577 --> 00:02:58,302
and they have to act on it.

72
00:02:58,302 --> 00:02:59,870
So make sure that you're writing down

73
00:02:59,870 --> 00:03:01,546
the things that show the scope,

74
00:03:01,546 --> 00:03:03,275
that show the effort, that show the work.

75
00:03:03,275 --> 00:03:05,260
Make the testing visible,
but not just make

76
00:03:05,260 --> 00:03:06,911
the testing visible make the testing

77
00:03:06,911 --> 00:03:09,918
visible such that it
acts as negative feedback

78
00:03:09,918 --> 00:03:12,412
in the process, so they know where

79
00:03:12,412 --> 00:03:14,995
the pain points are for testing.

80
00:03:17,667 --> 00:03:20,620
And this one is key and
slightly more controversial.

81
00:03:20,620 --> 00:03:23,338
Which is, don't do the work of others.

82
00:03:23,338 --> 00:03:24,760
Right, 'cause very often what happens

83
00:03:24,760 --> 00:03:26,892
is if you give an inch they'll take a mile

84
00:03:26,892 --> 00:03:28,500
and they'll keep taking the mile.

85
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:31,762
So, if you don't have enough time to test,

86
00:03:31,762 --> 00:03:35,707
that the software story is
delivered at the end of the day

87
00:03:35,707 --> 00:03:39,349
the testers might stay
late in order to test it.

88
00:03:39,349 --> 00:03:41,460
To provide the feedback
so that programmers

89
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:42,609
can work on it in the morning.

90
00:03:42,609 --> 00:03:43,668
Don't do that.

91
00:03:43,668 --> 00:03:45,088
Right, you're doing somebody else's work.

92
00:03:45,088 --> 00:03:47,603
You're absorbing their
failure in the system

93
00:03:47,603 --> 00:03:49,228
and taking it on board.

94
00:03:49,228 --> 00:03:51,478
They way they have no pain.

95
00:03:53,106 --> 00:03:55,470
They've got no negative
feedback causing them

96
00:03:55,470 --> 00:03:57,029
to change the process.

97
00:03:57,029 --> 00:03:59,667
Do not, absorb the work of others.

98
00:03:59,667 --> 00:04:02,610
Don't do the work of others.

99
00:04:02,610 --> 00:04:03,904
If we've agreed as a team,

100
00:04:03,904 --> 00:04:07,097
that everyone is going
to do automated execution

101
00:04:07,097 --> 00:04:10,115
and build test code, if
that's what we're calling it,

102
00:04:10,115 --> 00:04:11,863
then don't do that on your own.

103
00:04:11,863 --> 00:04:13,991
Bring people in and keep reminding people

104
00:04:13,991 --> 00:04:15,944
that we said we were
going to pair on this,

105
00:04:15,944 --> 00:04:18,620
can we pair on this
now, can we do this now.

106
00:04:18,620 --> 00:04:21,260
Keep pushing for that, make
opportunities for it to happen

107
00:04:21,260 --> 00:04:23,901
but control your part of the process.

108
00:04:23,901 --> 00:04:25,231
You don't attempt to fix Agile,

109
00:04:25,231 --> 00:04:28,178
you attempt to fix the bit
that you were involved in.

110
00:04:28,178 --> 00:04:30,532
(alarm)

111
00:04:30,532 --> 00:04:32,449
So that's four minutes.

112
00:04:34,417 --> 00:04:38,903
So I didn't quite cover
everything I've got written down.

113
00:04:38,903 --> 00:04:43,109
Other things I've got
written down, not much more.

114
00:04:43,109 --> 00:04:44,859
Part of the issue is,

115
00:04:46,339 --> 00:04:50,422
when we absorb other
peoples parts of the process

116
00:04:52,278 --> 00:04:56,486
we then can look bad because we are doing

117
00:04:56,486 --> 00:04:58,227
what we think it takes to make the project

118
00:04:58,227 --> 00:05:00,524
keep going on, but we're
getting enough time

119
00:05:00,524 --> 00:05:02,761
to do everything that we want to do.

120
00:05:02,761 --> 00:05:04,456
So we're not getting a chance to do

121
00:05:04,456 --> 00:05:05,929
all the testing that we want to do

122
00:05:05,929 --> 00:05:08,137
because we're trying to do
some automating as well.

123
00:05:08,137 --> 00:05:09,394
We're not getting the chance to do all

124
00:05:09,394 --> 00:05:10,769
the automating because
we're doing the testing.

125
00:05:10,769 --> 00:05:14,060
We're refining the stories late so we're

126
00:05:14,060 --> 00:05:16,101
having to go have meetings
about the acceptance

127
00:05:16,101 --> 00:05:17,911
criteria to figure out
what we need to test

128
00:05:17,911 --> 00:05:20,559
after the story has been played.

129
00:05:20,559 --> 00:05:22,678
Which then cuts into time that we could be

130
00:05:22,678 --> 00:05:25,761
using for testing, or automating.

131
00:05:25,761 --> 00:05:28,052
And the fact that we're absorbing all this

132
00:05:28,052 --> 00:05:30,236
makes the project look good.

133
00:05:30,236 --> 00:05:33,069
Makes the project run more
smoothly, which is important.

134
00:05:33,069 --> 00:05:35,673
It makes us look bad, and makes testing

135
00:05:35,673 --> 00:05:38,212
look bad on the project
because we're trying

136
00:05:38,212 --> 00:05:40,076
to do our best.

137
00:05:40,076 --> 00:05:43,826
And sometimes we have
to let the system fail,

138
00:05:44,827 --> 00:05:47,152
by fitting in with sustainable pace.

139
00:05:47,152 --> 00:05:50,152
Whatever is its 37.5, 40 hour weeks.

140
00:05:51,145 --> 00:05:54,538
To keep things on track,
but to make visible

141
00:05:54,538 --> 00:05:56,764
what we have done and not done.

142
00:05:56,764 --> 00:06:01,657
'Cause it's negative
feedback that causes change.

143
00:06:01,657 --> 00:06:03,957
I was thinking yesterday,
that a lot of this

144
00:06:03,957 --> 00:06:06,842
is coming from cybernetics
when I think about it.

145
00:06:06,842 --> 00:06:11,090
In terms of, we talk about
feedback a lot in Agile.

146
00:06:11,090 --> 00:06:14,946
So we have retrospectives,
we have standups.

147
00:06:14,946 --> 00:06:16,717
We have lots of interface
points that we're

148
00:06:16,717 --> 00:06:19,255
looking at feedback in order to come in.

149
00:06:19,255 --> 00:06:20,832
But then I realised yesterday,

150
00:06:20,832 --> 00:06:23,518
it's feedback in the sense that it's

151
00:06:23,518 --> 00:06:26,614
an output that comes in as input again.

152
00:06:26,614 --> 00:06:29,447
But, if the input is not processed

153
00:06:30,815 --> 00:06:32,552
it makes no difference.

154
00:06:32,552 --> 00:06:35,629
We say we're going to do certain things

155
00:06:35,629 --> 00:06:38,883
we have these goals that
comes in as feedback.

156
00:06:38,883 --> 00:06:41,365
If we don't act on it, it's irrelevant.

157
00:06:41,365 --> 00:06:42,774
We've filtered it out.

158
00:06:42,774 --> 00:06:44,990
It's not impacting the process.

159
00:06:44,990 --> 00:06:47,669
It doesn't cause the process to change.

160
00:06:47,669 --> 00:06:49,602
Which is why in retrospectives you

161
00:06:49,602 --> 00:06:51,551
don't just come out with goals.

162
00:06:51,551 --> 00:06:54,019
You come out with the next steps.

163
00:06:54,019 --> 00:06:55,476
And you do the next steps.

164
00:06:55,476 --> 00:06:56,931
And then you can remind yourselves

165
00:06:56,931 --> 00:07:01,061
during the actual sprint
about those next steps.

166
00:07:01,061 --> 00:07:02,030
Have we done this?

167
00:07:02,030 --> 00:07:03,099
You can do that in the standups.

168
00:07:03,099 --> 00:07:04,460
When are we going to do this?

169
00:07:04,460 --> 00:07:05,734
Do you have time today to do this?

170
00:07:05,734 --> 00:07:06,866
You can covert them into stories

171
00:07:06,866 --> 00:07:08,044
to make sure that you do them

172
00:07:08,044 --> 00:07:09,281
'cause they're on your board.

173
00:07:09,281 --> 00:07:11,498
There's steps that we can
take to make things visible,

174
00:07:11,498 --> 00:07:13,350
and make it actionable.

175
00:07:13,350 --> 00:07:16,683
That systems, only change when they have

176
00:07:18,006 --> 00:07:22,196
negative feedback coming
in that's processed.

177
00:07:22,196 --> 00:07:24,101
Very often what we have
is negative feedback

178
00:07:24,101 --> 00:07:27,004
that comes in that is filtered out

179
00:07:27,004 --> 00:07:29,993
or, and this is what often happens,

180
00:07:29,993 --> 00:07:34,480
to tester absorbed by a
single part of the system.

181
00:07:34,480 --> 00:07:36,895
So negative feedback comes in,

182
00:07:36,895 --> 00:07:39,786
that we don't have enough
time to do automating.

183
00:07:39,786 --> 00:07:43,583
That gets absorbed by the test part,

184
00:07:43,583 --> 00:07:45,550
so no one else gets
involved in the automating.

185
00:07:45,550 --> 00:07:47,698
Test part then has to work longer hours

186
00:07:47,698 --> 00:07:49,292
or weekends to try and get this done.

187
00:07:49,292 --> 00:07:51,476
Or it becomes testing stories that

188
00:07:51,476 --> 00:07:54,071
never get finished, they
look back, they look bad

189
00:07:54,071 --> 00:07:57,706
that part gets absorbed
by the testing part.

190
00:07:57,706 --> 00:07:59,918
And it shouldn't, an effective system

191
00:07:59,918 --> 00:08:03,404
doesn't have subsystems
absorbing negative feedback.

192
00:08:03,404 --> 00:08:06,390
Particularly when it's system feedback.

193
00:08:06,390 --> 00:08:08,752
And most of the feedback
on an Agile project

194
00:08:08,752 --> 00:08:10,551
is system level 'cause it's the system

195
00:08:10,551 --> 00:08:12,271
that has to change, it's
the way that we develop.

196
00:08:12,271 --> 00:08:17,271
It's the way that we work as
a team, that has to change.

197
00:08:17,371 --> 00:08:21,570
And until that feedback
get absorbed as the system,

198
00:08:21,570 --> 00:08:23,776
the process doesn't change.

199
00:08:23,776 --> 00:08:25,315
Which is why its hard for individuals

200
00:08:25,315 --> 00:08:28,673
to fix it because individuals
take that feedback

201
00:08:28,673 --> 00:08:31,721
and absorb it on their own.

202
00:08:31,721 --> 00:08:34,449
Now ideally, in a four minute talk

203
00:08:34,449 --> 00:08:36,124
I would like to mention that as well

204
00:08:36,124 --> 00:08:37,562
but then people have to understand

205
00:08:37,562 --> 00:08:39,077
the notions of cybernetics.

206
00:08:39,077 --> 00:08:40,912
And I'm concerned that people might

207
00:08:40,912 --> 00:08:42,341
not understand cybernetics or feedback.

208
00:08:42,341 --> 00:08:43,618
So I'm going to talk about it more

209
00:08:43,618 --> 00:08:46,048
general clear actions steps you can do.

210
00:08:46,048 --> 00:08:48,910
To me that's coming from the notion

211
00:08:48,910 --> 00:08:53,577
of cybernetics and systems
viewpoint of how people work.

